### 2026-08-14T02:56:09.728Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

No stale doc references. Let me answer your questions directly, now confirmed by passing tests:

1. **Is anything still special about pasted debris vs. a regular falling piece?** No — as of this pass, both fall and lock into the pile through the same physics, and both get detected by the same per-tick line-check.
2. **Does everything above a cleared row fall debris-style, or something special?** Now debris-style — I removed the old instant `dropRowsAbove` shift entirely. Clearing a line just deletes it; whatever's above falls naturally, one row per tick, through the same `settleFloatingCellsStep()` debris uses.
3. **Your hole-filling scenario** — tested literally (paste via real header buttons, resume, then run ticks): the row clears on the very first tick after resuming, and the cell that was sitting above cascades down correctly (landing one column over from directly-above, because of the hex "straight down" zigzag — expected geometry, not a bug).

The only real bug this round was that **line-clear detection itself only ran on piece-lock**, never on debris settling — so a row completed by paste (or by any future non-piece source) would sit there uncleared. Fixed: `checkForClears()` now runs every tick, same as settling.


